{
  // The API's typecheck program: production source AND the whole test tree.
  //
  // ## Why this file exists at all
  //
  // `tsconfig.json` is the BUILD config. Its `include` is `src/**/*.ts` and it
  // must stay that way: it emits to `dist/`, and a test file compiled into the
  // production image is a test file shipped to production.
  //
  // `api:typecheck` used to run that same build config with `--noEmit`, which
  // meant the 57 files under `apps/api/test` -- 52 of them `*.pg-spec.ts` --
  // were never statically checked by anything. ts-jest compiled each one for
  // the first time at RUN TIME, inside the real-PostgreSQL suite. A stale
  // import therefore surfaced roughly 36 minutes into a database run while
  // `pnpm typecheck` reported success in seconds; V3.3 Story #58 (`#58a`) hit
  // exactly that, on a hook rename (bug #97).
  //
  // Every other project in the workspace keeps its specs beside the code in
  // `src/`, so `src/**/*.ts` already covers them. `apps/api` is the only one
  // whose tests live outside `src`, and it was the only one they were invisible
  // to. `apps/web` has no such gap either -- its config already spans its own
  // `test/` directory.
  //
  // ## Why a second config rather than widening the first
  //
  // Widening `tsconfig.json` to `test/**/*.ts` would make `api:build` emit the
  // test tree into `dist/`. Adding an `exclude` to compensate would then leave
  // the build's include and exclude disagreeing about the same glob, which is
  // the shape that quietly drifts. Two files, each with one job, cannot.
  //
  // ## Why the name is not `tsconfig.test.json`
  //
  // `apps/web/tsconfig.test.json` already exists and is the OPPOSITE kind of
  // thing: an emitting config (`noEmit: false`) used to compile tests. Reusing
  // that filename here for a non-emitting whole-program check would make the
  // two mean different things in the same repository.
  //
  // ## What it does NOT do
  //
  // It declares no `paths`, no `types` and no compiler flag of its own beyond
  // `noEmit`. Everything else is inherited from `tsconfig.json` and through it
  // from `tsconfig.base.json` -- including `strict`, `types: ["jest", "node"]`
  // and the single path map. A second path table here would be one more thing
  // to drift, which is the very bug `runtime-path-map.spec.ts` exists to catch.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    // Checking only. `outDir` and `rootDir` are inherited and inert under
    // `noEmit`; the build config remains the only thing that writes `dist/`.
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "test/**/*.ts"],
  // `tsconfig.json` excludes `**/*.spec.ts` so the production build does not
  // SHIP them. `exclude` is inherited through `extends`, so without this line
  // that build-time decision would silently stop the seven `src/**/*.spec.ts
  // files being type-checked at all -- trading one blind spot for another.
  //
  // Empty, not absent: an absent `exclude` inherits the parent`s.
  "exclude": []
}
